  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold continental air mass along with a strong surface high \nremains in place today. Clear skies and calm winds overnight has\nlead to pockets of lows in the 30s while the rest of the region\nfell into the low 40s. Today will be similar to yesterday \nthough some more high clouds sneak into the Northeast as a \ncoastal low deepens off the coast of the Carolinas. Dry air in \nplace and a similar set up tonight as last night, temperatures \nwill drop off with lows falling to almost identical values as \nthis morning. Some patchy frost is possible in elevated deeper \nnarrow river valleys of the Twin Tiers, Catskills, and Southern \nTug Hill. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nModels continue to slowly hone in on the evolution of the \nNoreaster this weekend. Trends overnight have concentrated the \nlow center a little farther to the west with the low occluding \noff the Mid Atlantic coast rather than off the New England \ncoast. Precipitation chances were raised in the Catskills but \nthey were not extended west too much as dry air from the strong \nhigh pressure to the west will help dry out the back side of the\nprecipitation shield. Winds look to be breezy Saturday and \nSunday with northerly winds of 10 to 15 mph and gusts up to 25 \nmph. Ensemble spreads in the low pressure centers is still very \nhigh given how close we are to the system being near by but even\nthe farthest west solution would keep the heaviest rain and \nstrongest winds east of the Catskills. Overall impacts to our \nregion will be minimal with this noreaster.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nOnce the noreaster occludes and moves out to sea Sunday night \ninto Monday, upper level ridging builds back in. Southwest flow \nbrings in a milder air mass aloft with a gradual warm up \nexpected into mid week. Subsidence aloft along with moisture \nreturn taking its time post noreaster, precipitation chances are\nlow to none Monday through Wednesday.",
